;; host-surface-renames.rs.intueri — the names `src/host/` must now speak. Rust identifiers, not a
;; wat namespace (hence `.rs.intueri`); the house shape kept — anchor vocabulary, the PROMISE each
;; name must keep, candidates each with its own counter-argument.
;;
;; DESIGN: docs/arc/2026/06/255-builtin-registry/DESIGN-STONE-the-host-surface-renames.md
;; PRIOR CAST: rust-entry-surface-naming.rs.intueri (0c1667524) — named `src/host/` on two
;;             independent first-place votes, and cut panic_hook.rs from the family.
;;
;; ── ANCHOR VOCABULARY (already spoken; the new names must live beside these) ──────────────────
;;   src/host/          compose.rs · harness.rs · test_runner.rs      ← the home, ruled by the ward
;;   src/edn/           render · bridge · contract · error
;;   src/load/          loader · stdlib · source
;;   :user::main        wat's OWN entry point (arc 170). NOT this surface.
;;   FrozenWorld        what `from_source` produces and `run` invokes against (src/freeze.rs)
;;   wat::main!         the proc-macro whose GENERATED TEXT names these identifiers
;;   wat_test!          ditto
;;
;;   ⚠ THE <Verb>Outcome FAMILY — measured, and it is large and consistent:
;;     RecvOutcome 195 · SelectOutcome 100 · SendOutcome 79 · ReadFrameOutcome 56
;;     CosineOutcome 46 · AcceptOutcome 43 · CloseOutcome 41 · ConnectOutcome 38
;;     VectorDecodeOutcome 33 · DeftestOutcome 33 · ReadlnOutcome · ReadOutcome · FormOutcome
;;   Every member carries a VERB. Harness's is bare `Outcome`, re-exported to the crate root, so an
;;   external caller writes `wat::Outcome` among a dozen verbed siblings.
;;
;; ══ TARGET 1 — the facade type, today `Harness` (LEVEL 1 LIE per the prior cast) ══════════════
;; PROMISE it must keep: it holds a FrozenWorld built from wat source and invokes `:user::main`
;; from a host Rust process, returning a typed result. It is NOT test infrastructure — its own doc
;; must currently fence that off ("A test runner. That's `wat test <path>`"), which is the evidence
;; it fails Obvious. It is the thing an EXTERNAL crate writes: `use wat::???;`
;;   candidates:  Guest · Embed · Program · Instance · Runner · Session · Loaded · Wat
;;   counter-arguments, so the ward weighs a real trade:
;;     Guest    — pairs with the home (`host` runs a `guest`), but is "guest" spoken anywhere else
;;                in this codebase, or is it a metaphor arriving with the name?
;;     Embed    — the file's OWN verb ("embed wat as a sub-language"), BUT `embed` was KILLED as the
;;                home name for over-promising; reusing it re-imports that argument one level down
;;     Program  — accurate and plain, but `:user::main` is also "the program"; two senses again
;;     Instance — interpreter-embedding term of art (cf. wasm), but generic
;;     Runner   — collides with test_runner.rs, a sibling IN THIS HOME
;;     Session  — implies statefulness across calls that this type does not have
;;     Loaded   — an adjective where every sibling type is a noun
;;
;; ══ TARGET 2 — its error, today `HarnessError` ════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; PROMISE: the typed failure of building-or-invoking. Follows TARGET 1 mechanically UNLESS the ward
;; sees a reason it should not. Note the house has both shapes: `LoadError` / `StdlibError` /
;; `ConfigError` (noun+Error) and `EdnReadError` (verb-phrase+Error).
;;
;; ══ TARGET 3 — the result struct, today `Outcome` ═════════════════════════════════════════════
;; PROMISE: what `run` hands back — today `{stdout, stderr}`, both ALWAYS EMPTY (a no-op pending arc
;; 170 slice 1f; the doc now says so). Its own surface is 2 sites; the crate-root re-export is the
;; problem. THE QUESTION IS WHICH VERB. `RunOutcome`? `<Target1>Outcome`? Or does it leave the
;; family entirely — is it an Outcome at all, given every sibling names a fallible I/O act and this
;; one names "the program finished"?
;;   ⚠ Do not assume it must join the family. Ask whether it belongs to it.
;;
;; ══ TARGET 4 — the free functions, today `compose_and_run` / `compose_and_run_with_loader` ═════
;; PROMISE: assemble the frozen world from user source + dep sources + dep Rust shims, wire it to
;; REAL OS stdio and REAL OS signals (SIGINT/TERM/USR1/USR2/HUP), invoke `:user::main`. It is what
;; `wat::main!` expands to. The prior cast's reader called `compose` a mumble because the verb was
;; compounded EVERY time it was used, and because the OS-wiring half is unnamed by it. That reader
;; nominated `bootstrap` / `launch` for the ACTIVITY.
;;   candidates:  run · launch · boot · bootstrap · main · start · run_program
;;   counter-arguments:
;;     run        — collides with `Harness::run`, a method on TARGET 1 in the same home
;;     launch     — reads well; is it distinguishable from `spawn`, which this codebase already
;;                  spends on process/thread creation (`spawn-program`, `spawn-process`)?
;;     boot       — `src/time.rs` already says "Process boot clock"; a second sense of boot
;;     bootstrap  — precise about the activity, but generic the way `api` is
;;     main       — `wat::main!` the macro vs `wat::main` the fn: two things one word
;;     start      — pairs with nothing; what is `stop`?
;;
;; ══ TARGET 5 — the file names, today `harness.rs` and `compose.rs` ════════════════════════════
;; PROMISE: name what the file holds, sitting beside `test_runner.rs` in `src/host/`. A file may
;; keep its name even if its TYPE renames — say so if that is the honest answer.
;;
;;   src/host/???.rs   <- the facade (Harness) · src/host/???.rs   <- the OS-wiring entry (compose)

;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; ⊘ CAST 2026-08-25 — three casts: harness.rs · compose.rs · the <Verb>Outcome vocabulary.
;;   None saw the others. Builder ruled `guest.rs`, and `entry.rs` under host/.
;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;;   Harness              -> Guest          src/host/harness.rs -> src/host/guest.rs
;;   HarnessError         -> GuestError
;;   Outcome              -> RunOutput
;;   compose_and_run      -> run_program    src/host/compose.rs -> src/host/entry.rs
;;   compose_and_run_with_loader -> run_program_with_loader
;;
;; ── TARGET 1 `Guest` — the ward FALSIFIED ITS OWN COUNTER-ARGUMENT FROM THE DISK ──────────────
;;   The framing asked: "is `guest` spoken anywhere else in this codebase, or is it a metaphor
;;   arriving with the name?" Answer, found one file up: src/host/mod.rs:4 —
;;       "the `Harness` facade for embedding wat as a GUEST in a Rust process"
;;   Written hours earlier by HOME #7's own rider. `host` names the directory; `guest` was ALREADY
;;   the word for what lives inside it. The counter-argument I wrote was answerable from the disk
;;   and I had not looked.
;;   Rejected with evidence: Program (this 228-line file uses "the wat program" in TWO live senses
;;   — :user::main at :199/:201 and the struct itself at :58); Runner (test_runner.rs is a literal
;;   sibling); Session (each run() is independent against an immutable world — no session state);
;;   Instance (generic in a crate saturated with unrelated "instance" senses); Embed (already
;;   killed one level up; reusing it repeats the objection rather than answering it); Loaded (an
;;   adjective where every sibling type is a noun).
;;
;; ── TARGET 3 `RunOutput` — AND THE PREMISE WAS WRONG IN A BETTER DIRECTION ────────────────────
;;   I framed `Outcome` as "the only VERBLESS member of the family." Both casts destroyed that
;;   independently: IT IS NOT A MEMBER AT ALL.
;;
;;   The family, defined precisely by the vocabulary cast (and it NAMES ITSELF — types.rs:1423,
;;   1789, 1889, 1940 say "the recv'-outcome wall", "the send'-outcome wall"):
;;     THE MATCHABLE, DISCRIMINATED RESULT OF ONE PREVIOUSLY-RAISING OPERATION, converted so
;;     failure is a VALUE instead of an unwind. Membership = enum + verb + replaces a raise +
;;     variants the caller MUST branch on + purity derived from the payload.
;;   harness::Outcome has NONE of the five: a plain struct of two Vec<String>, already inside
;;   Result<Outcome, HarnessError> so fallibility is handled OUTSIDE it, both fields ALWAYS EMPTY,
;;   and ZERO consumers beyond its own definition, ctor, and the lib.rs re-export.
;;
;;   ★★ AND THIS WARD ALREADY RULED ON THIS EXACT NAME, A MONTH AGO. src/types.rs:1543-1547:
;;       "Named by an INTUERI CAST (2026-07-28) ... and a bare `Outcome` would read ambiguously
;;        beside `:wat::service::Outcome` in the defservice handler that is its first consumer."
;;   FormOutcome carries its verb BECAUSE OF THAT RULING. And src/lib.rs:142 re-exports a bare
;;   `Outcome` to the crate root anyway. Naming it RunOutcome would have been a WORSE lie than
;;   leaving it: false resemblance to a dozen enums, promising branches a struct cannot have.
;;
;; ── TARGET 4/5 `run_program` / `entry.rs` — EVERY REJECT DIED ON A GREP-VERIFIED COLLISION ────
;;   launch     ThreadLaunch / ProcessLaunch (spawn.rs:445,531,834,1090) — already the NOUN for
;;              "what you get once something has been spawned". Confirmed collision, not a risk.
;;   bootstrap  bootstrap_wat_vm_process (freeze.rs:262) is IN THIS VERY CALL CHAIN — called at
;;              freeze.rs:1452 from the orchestrator compose_and_run_with_loader reaches.
;;              host::bootstrap() would call freeze::bootstrap_wat_vm_process() two frames down.
;;   boot       collides TWICE: time.rs's "Process boot clock" and src/process/boot/'s "boot wire".
;;   start      start-primed-stdio (freeze.rs:306-345), same call chain.
;;   run        the module doc's own paragraph exists to explain "why this isn't just `.run()`" —
;;              naming it `run` makes that sentence self-undermining.
;;   run_program is the CLI's OWN established term: "Program mode" (distribution/mod.rs:77-90).
;;   entry.rs   from the file's own section header: "The entry point `wat::main!` expands to".
;;              ⚠ NOTE THE TENSION, RULED BY THE BUILDER: the FIRST round rejected `entry` as a
;;              HOME name because arc 170 owns "entry point" for :user::main. As a FILE name under
;;              src/host/, the home supplies the context a bare src/entry/ lacked. Same word,
;;              opposite rulings, and the difference is the scope it sits in.
;;
;; ── ⛔ A THIRD LYING DOC — AND MY OWN FRAMING REPEATED IT ─────────────────────────────────────
;;   compose.rs:25 asserts the fn wires `crate::io::RealStdin`/`RealStdout`/`RealStderr` directly
;;   onto the frozen world. That doc line is THE ONLY OCCURRENCE OF `crate::io` IN THE FILE. The
;;   body (:192-199) says the Real* IO construction "retires alongside the four-arg main_args
;;   plumbing" — arc 170 slice 1f's substrate services own fd 0/1/2 now.
;;   ★ AND THE PROMISE I WROTE INTO TARGET 4 ABOVE — "wire it to REAL OS stdio and REAL OS
;;     signals" — WAS COPIED FROM THAT STALE DOC. Only the SIGNAL half is true. The question I
;;     asked was built on the lie the ward was sent to look past. It caught it anyway.
;;   Third lying doc in this family: Harness::run's, test_runner's discovery rule, and this.
